00:00:00,730 --> 00:00:02,820
Here is a mammoth with a mystery

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:04,380
written into its DNA.

3
00:00:04,810 --> 00:00:07,340
Two changes in a gene called FOX

4
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:10,260
Q one led scientists to predict a

5
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:12,410
satin-like coat. They did not see

6
00:00:12,410 --> 00:00:14,650
the coat, and a prediction about hair

7
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,800
is not proof that this animal lost

8
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:18,370
its wool or insulation.

9
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:20,670
It lived on Wrangel Island in the

10
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:22,780
Arctic, thousands of years ago.

11
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:24,290
Now the strange part.

12
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:26,890
A later study of many mammoth genomes

13
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:29,880
suggested that the isolated population had
recovered

14
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:31,480
after a severe bottleneck.

15
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:34,380
Recovery and genetic strain could both be

16
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:37,560
true. This story comes from evidence, not

17
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:40,080
anyone who watched the last mammoth die.

18
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:42,490
Bones preserve chemical signatures;

19
00:00:42,930 --> 00:00:45,810
ancient DNA preserves fragments of
inheritance.

20
00:00:46,210 --> 00:00:49,430
Archaeological sites preserve traces of
people, with

21
00:00:49,430 --> 00:00:52,410
gaps between them. Each witness answers a

22
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:55,090
different question. So how did a
population

23
00:00:55,090 --> 00:00:57,980
that survived for thousands of years
disappear?

24
00:00:58,410 --> 00:00:59,780
We have four witnesses:

25
00:01:00,210 --> 00:01:05,110
a 2017 genome comparison, a 2019 study

26
00:01:05,110 --> 00:01:09,180
of chemistry and environment, the
archaeological record

27
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:12,730
of humans, and a larger 2024 genome

28
00:01:12,730 --> 00:01:14,360
study. We will ask what each can

29
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:17,830
actually tell us, what remains plausible,
and

30
00:01:17,830 --> 00:01:21,340
what is missing. This is a cold

31
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:23,820
case, not a promise of a single

32
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:24,760
convicted killer.

33
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:28,700
Around ten thousand years ago, sea levels

34
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:31,240
rose and Arctic coastlines changed.

35
00:01:31,670 --> 00:01:34,930
A mammoth population became isolated on
Wrangel

36
00:01:34,930 --> 00:01:37,250
Island, with no easy route back to

37
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:39,730
the mainland. They lived there for
thousands

38
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:42,530
of years, among the last mammoths on

39
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:46,130
Earth. Other populations disappeared
earlier and under

40
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:49,420
different conditions. On the East European
plains,

41
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:53,020
mammoths vanished roughly fifteen thousand
years ago.

42
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:56,870
On St. Paul Island in Alaska, they

43
00:01:56,870 --> 00:01:59,390
lasted until about fifty six hundred years

44
00:01:59,390 --> 00:02:02,430
ago. Isotopic changes in remains from
different

45
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:05,410
regions can give researchers clues about
changes

46
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:08,020
in diet and environment, although the
details

47
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:09,420
are not the same everywhere.

48
00:02:09,810 --> 00:02:12,580
Picture an island population with no easy

49
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:15,220
way to exchange animals or genes with

50
00:02:15,220 --> 00:02:19,270
mainland herds. That isolation does not
automatically

51
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:21,760
doom it. The population persisted through
a

52
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,840
long stretch of changing Arctic history,
although

53
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,930
the surviving samples cannot tell us what

54
00:02:26,930 --> 00:02:28,040
happened in every year.

55
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:31,200
Wrangel's sampled bones offered a
different signal.

56
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:35,000
The 2019 study found carbon and nitrogen

57
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,090
values in collagen broadly stable across
the

58
00:02:38,090 --> 00:02:41,120
sampled period. That is evidence about
diet,

59
00:02:41,510 --> 00:02:43,820
not proof that every season was favourable

60
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:45,720
or that no animal went hungry.

61
00:02:46,220 --> 00:02:48,810
A population endured isolation for
millennia.

62
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:51,760
Its final disappearance remains
unexplained.

63
00:02:52,975 --> 00:02:54,985
The first witness is DNA.

64
00:02:55,395 --> 00:02:59,225
In 2017, researchers compared two woolly
mammoth

65
00:02:59,225 --> 00:03:02,865
genomes: one from mainland Siberia around
forty

66
00:03:02,865 --> 00:03:05,265
five thousand years old, and one from

67
00:03:05,265 --> 00:03:07,885
Wrangel Island around forty three hundred
years

68
00:03:07,885 --> 00:03:10,615
old. Two individuals are not a complete

69
00:03:10,615 --> 00:03:12,565
record of either population.

70
00:03:12,845 --> 00:03:14,815
The island genome carried an excess of

71
00:03:14,815 --> 00:03:16,545
changes that could disrupt genes:

72
00:03:16,985 --> 00:03:20,605
more deletions, more deletions affecting
genes, and

73
00:03:20,605 --> 00:03:22,225
more premature stop signals.

74
00:03:22,525 --> 00:03:25,395
The estimated effective population size
was more

75
00:03:25,395 --> 00:03:27,235
than forty three times lower than the

76
00:03:27,235 --> 00:03:30,365
mainland animal's estimate. That number is
a

77
00:03:30,365 --> 00:03:32,695
genetic estimate, not a head count.

78
00:03:32,995 --> 00:03:35,585
A premature stop can interrupt the
instructions

79
00:03:35,585 --> 00:03:38,495
for a protein. Deletions can remove pieces

80
00:03:38,495 --> 00:03:40,855
of the genome. The study also reported

81
00:03:40,855 --> 00:03:43,935
losses affecting some smell-related
receptors and

82
00:03:43,935 --> 00:03:47,625
urinary proteins; their biological
consequences for this

83
00:03:47,625 --> 00:03:50,165
individual are not known from the sequence

84
00:03:50,165 --> 00:03:52,965
alone. The researchers interpreted the
pattern as

85
00:03:52,965 --> 00:03:54,915
consistent with genomic meltdown.

86
00:03:55,175 --> 00:03:58,165
In a small population, natural selection
can

87
00:03:58,165 --> 00:04:00,815
become less effective at removing harmful
variants.

88
00:04:01,235 --> 00:04:03,345
But a comparison of two genomes could

89
00:04:03,345 --> 00:04:06,075
not establish that genetic damage caused
extinction.

90
00:04:06,435 --> 00:04:08,625
One detail made the stakes feel especially

91
00:04:08,625 --> 00:04:10,065
close to the animal itself.

92
00:04:11,137 --> 00:04:14,337
The Wrangel mammoth's genome contained two
independent

93
00:04:14,337 --> 00:04:16,577
loss of function mutations in FOX Q

94
00:04:16,577 --> 00:04:19,447
one. From comparisons with other animals,
the

95
00:04:19,447 --> 00:04:21,847
researchers predicted a satin-like coat.

96
00:04:22,097 --> 00:04:24,007
They did not observe the coat, and

97
00:04:24,007 --> 00:04:25,967
this prediction does not prove the animal

98
00:04:25,967 --> 00:04:29,107
lost its wool or insulation, or froze.

99
00:04:29,437 --> 00:04:31,677
That difference between a molecular trace
and

100
00:04:31,677 --> 00:04:33,257
a living trait matters.

101
00:04:33,677 --> 00:04:35,767
DNA can preserve a clue when the

102
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:38,037
animal itself is gone, but it cannot

103
00:04:38,037 --> 00:04:40,487
always tell us exactly how the animal

104
00:04:40,487 --> 00:04:43,267
looked or coped with Arctic conditions.

105
00:04:43,687 --> 00:04:47,227
The 2017 result raised a warning about

106
00:04:47,227 --> 00:04:49,517
the genetic effects of isolation.

107
00:04:49,927 --> 00:04:51,867
But it came from one island genome

108
00:04:51,867 --> 00:04:53,837
compared with one mainland genome.

109
00:04:54,157 --> 00:04:56,617
Seven years later, a larger study would

110
00:04:56,617 --> 00:04:59,277
find both a severe bottleneck and a

111
00:04:59,277 --> 00:05:03,267
surprising recovery, without removing the
possibility of

112
00:05:03,267 --> 00:05:04,667
lasting genetic strain.

113
00:05:05,811 --> 00:05:07,941
The second witness is chemistry.

114
00:05:08,351 --> 00:05:11,651
Carbon and nitrogen isotopes in collagen
offer

115
00:05:11,651 --> 00:05:15,081
clues about diet; sulfur and strontium can

116
00:05:15,081 --> 00:05:17,591
also provide evidence about water and
local

117
00:05:17,591 --> 00:05:21,941
geology. In 2019 researchers compared
remains from

118
00:05:21,941 --> 00:05:23,991
Wrangel and other northern regions.

119
00:05:24,371 --> 00:05:27,061
The Wrangel carbon and nitrogen values
remained

120
00:05:27,061 --> 00:05:29,401
broadly stable in the samples they
studied.

121
00:05:29,861 --> 00:05:32,721
That weighs against some gradual dietary
changes.

122
00:05:33,251 --> 00:05:34,681
It does not prove the herd was

123
00:05:34,681 --> 00:05:37,121
healthy or that the island always offered

124
00:05:37,121 --> 00:05:40,201
easy conditions. Isotope measurements are
records of

125
00:05:40,201 --> 00:05:42,961
particular animals and tissues, not a
weather

126
00:05:42,961 --> 00:05:44,751
station reporting every storm.

127
00:05:44,931 --> 00:05:48,221
A stable sampled dietary signal can
coexist

128
00:05:48,221 --> 00:05:50,851
with seasonal stress, and a rapid event

129
00:05:50,851 --> 00:05:52,661
could escape a record that is sparse

130
00:05:52,661 --> 00:05:55,891
in time. Sulfur and strontium patterns
were

131
00:05:55,891 --> 00:05:59,181
interpreted as consistent with increased
bedrock weathering

132
00:05:59,181 --> 00:06:01,041
toward the end of the island population's

133
00:06:01,041 --> 00:06:03,591
history. The authors suggested this might
have

134
00:06:03,591 --> 00:06:05,461
affected drinking water quality.

135
00:06:05,831 --> 00:06:08,081
It is an inference, not a directly

136
00:06:08,081 --> 00:06:09,841
tested glass of ancient water.

137
00:06:10,191 --> 00:06:12,091
They also proposed a rapid threat:

138
00:06:12,531 --> 00:06:15,171
rain falling on snow and freezing, making

139
00:06:15,171 --> 00:06:16,261
plants hard to reach.

140
00:06:16,671 --> 00:06:18,571
An icing event could harm a small

141
00:06:18,571 --> 00:06:20,731
herd quickly. But no one observed a

142
00:06:20,731 --> 00:06:24,501
fatal Wrangel winter. Genetic strain,
water and

143
00:06:24,501 --> 00:06:26,531
weather may have interacted;

144
00:06:26,981 --> 00:06:29,801
that scenario remains a hypothesis, not a

145
00:06:29,801 --> 00:06:30,681
solved case.

146
00:06:31,817 --> 00:06:33,487
The third witness is us.

147
00:06:33,957 --> 00:06:36,397
People hunted mammoths elsewhere, so it is

148
00:06:36,397 --> 00:06:39,007
reasonable to ask whether humans reached
Wrangel

149
00:06:39,277 --> 00:06:40,857
while the animals were still alive.

150
00:06:41,417 --> 00:06:44,027
The earliest known archaeological evidence
of people

151
00:06:44,027 --> 00:06:46,037
on the island dates to around thirty

152
00:06:46,037 --> 00:06:49,367
six hundred calibrated years before
present, several

153
00:06:49,367 --> 00:06:52,227
centuries after the youngest known mammoth
remains.

154
00:06:52,627 --> 00:06:54,967
The earliest evidence is not necessarily
the

155
00:06:54,967 --> 00:06:59,297
first arrival. The 2019 researchers
cautioned that

156
00:06:59,297 --> 00:07:01,967
a human contribution could not be ruled

157
00:07:01,967 --> 00:07:05,037
out, because direct evidence of an
encounter

158
00:07:05,367 --> 00:07:06,797
would be difficult to find.

159
00:07:07,267 --> 00:07:10,557
Elsewhere, archaeological finds show that
humans and

160
00:07:10,557 --> 00:07:13,917
mammoths interacted, but evidence from
another place

161
00:07:14,187 --> 00:07:16,977
cannot automatically be transferred to
this island.

162
00:07:17,227 --> 00:07:18,657
Here we have to keep the known

163
00:07:18,657 --> 00:07:21,217
dates separate from possibilities that the
record

164
00:07:21,217 --> 00:07:24,147
has not resolved. So human involvement is

165
00:07:24,147 --> 00:07:26,417
possible, but the known dates do not

166
00:07:26,417 --> 00:07:28,857
establish it. The apparent gap is not

167
00:07:28,857 --> 00:07:31,117
proof of innocence, and it is not

168
00:07:31,117 --> 00:07:32,427
proof of hunting either.

169
00:07:32,907 --> 00:07:35,037
Three witnesses have offered clues;

170
00:07:35,467 --> 00:07:37,487
none has shown the final moment.

171
00:07:38,437 --> 00:07:42,297
In 2024, researchers published a wider
genetic

172
00:07:42,297 --> 00:07:46,287
record: twenty one Siberian woolly mammoth
genomes,

173
00:07:46,717 --> 00:07:50,417
including seven mainland animals from
before isolation

174
00:07:50,857 --> 00:07:53,197
and fourteen from the Wrangel population.

175
00:07:53,627 --> 00:07:55,427
The bottleneck was severe.

176
00:07:55,857 --> 00:08:00,097
Heterozygosity, one measure of genetic
diversity, fell

177
00:08:00,097 --> 00:08:03,187
by more than forty percent after
isolation.

178
00:08:03,567 --> 00:08:06,927
Long runs of homozygosity, stretches with
matching

179
00:08:06,927 --> 00:08:10,267
inherited copies, rose from roughly ten
percent

180
00:08:10,537 --> 00:08:12,157
to about forty two percent of the

181
00:08:12,157 --> 00:08:16,107
genome. Diversity in the major
histocompatibility complex,

182
00:08:16,547 --> 00:08:18,307
a group of genes involved in immune

183
00:08:18,307 --> 00:08:21,987
responses, declined more than genome-wide
diversity.

184
00:08:22,457 --> 00:08:25,517
Homozygosity and genetic diversity measure
related but

185
00:08:25,517 --> 00:08:27,207
different aspects of the genome.

186
00:08:27,727 --> 00:08:29,437
Neither is a direct count of sick

187
00:08:29,437 --> 00:08:32,297
animals. The loss of immune-gene diversity

188
00:08:32,297 --> 00:08:34,937
is a genetic finding, not a diagnosis

189
00:08:34,937 --> 00:08:36,237
of a specific infection.

190
00:08:36,587 --> 00:08:37,707
Then came the surprise.

191
00:08:38,117 --> 00:08:41,427
Modelling suggested that effective
population size fell

192
00:08:41,427 --> 00:08:43,257
to eight or fewer at the bottleneck,

193
00:08:43,727 --> 00:08:45,987
then recovered to around two hundred to

194
00:08:45,987 --> 00:08:48,857
three hundred within roughly twenty
generations.

195
00:08:49,327 --> 00:08:52,357
Remember: effective size is a genetic
model

196
00:08:52,357 --> 00:08:55,327
parameter, not a census of eight mammoths

197
00:08:55,327 --> 00:08:58,507
becoming three hundred. Across the years
represented

198
00:08:58,507 --> 00:09:01,807
by the samples, the population appeared
comparatively

199
00:09:01,807 --> 00:09:04,867
stable. Recovery did not mean its genetic

200
00:09:04,867 --> 00:09:06,297
risks disappeared.

201
00:09:07,433 --> 00:09:10,193
Diversity and homozygosity are not the
only

202
00:09:10,193 --> 00:09:11,883
things researchers can compare.

203
00:09:12,283 --> 00:09:14,863
They can also examine variants predicted
to

204
00:09:14,863 --> 00:09:17,573
be harmful. But ancient genomes differ in

205
00:09:17,573 --> 00:09:19,433
how much of their sequence is readable,

206
00:09:19,713 --> 00:09:21,883
so coverage and data quality matter.

207
00:09:22,293 --> 00:09:24,693
Imagine looking into the same room through

208
00:09:24,693 --> 00:09:26,553
windows of different sizes.

209
00:09:27,003 --> 00:09:29,523
The larger window might reveal more damage

210
00:09:29,523 --> 00:09:31,183
even if neither room is worse.

211
00:09:31,723 --> 00:09:34,123
The comparison matters because a shallow
sequence

212
00:09:34,443 --> 00:09:36,583
may miss changes that are visible in

213
00:09:36,583 --> 00:09:37,813
a better-covered genome.

214
00:09:38,173 --> 00:09:40,853
Without testing that bias, a difference in

215
00:09:40,853 --> 00:09:42,613
the data could be mistaken for a

216
00:09:42,613 --> 00:09:44,143
difference between the animals.

217
00:09:44,683 --> 00:09:47,833
After accounting for these differences,
the 2024

218
00:09:47,833 --> 00:09:49,703
team found two opposing trends.

219
00:09:49,703 --> 00:09:53,213
Variants predicted to have the highest
impact

220
00:09:53,443 --> 00:09:56,673
became less common, consistent with
natural selection

221
00:09:56,673 --> 00:09:57,623
purging some of them.

222
00:09:58,113 --> 00:10:02,083
Moderately harmful variants accumulated,
consistent with genetic

223
00:10:02,083 --> 00:10:05,903
drift outpacing selection. These are
predicted effects,

224
00:10:06,363 --> 00:10:08,963
not medical examinations of individual
mammoths.

225
00:10:09,313 --> 00:10:11,423
The sampled genomes did not show a

226
00:10:11,423 --> 00:10:14,713
clear accelerating genomic collapse across
their history.

227
00:10:15,203 --> 00:10:17,983
Yet the youngest genome predates the final

228
00:10:17,983 --> 00:10:21,763
generations. A population can recover
demographically and

229
00:10:21,763 --> 00:10:23,833
still carry lasting genetic strain.

230
00:10:24,283 --> 00:10:27,313
Neither finding, by itself, tells us what

231
00:10:27,313 --> 00:10:28,503
caused the extinction.

232
00:10:29,574 --> 00:10:31,474
Here is the missing witness.

233
00:10:31,914 --> 00:10:36,594
The 2024 genome dataset contains no
samples

234
00:10:36,724 --> 00:10:40,004
from the final ten generations, roughly
three

235
00:10:40,004 --> 00:10:43,434
centuries. Its youngest genome dates to
around

236
00:10:43,434 --> 00:10:46,644
forty three hundred calibrated years
before present.

237
00:10:47,084 --> 00:10:49,034
That gap is in the genomic dataset;

238
00:10:49,504 --> 00:10:50,774
it does not mean there is no

239
00:10:50,774 --> 00:10:52,294
other evidence from that period.

240
00:10:52,774 --> 00:10:55,294
A newer lead shows why careful questions

241
00:10:55,294 --> 00:10:59,534
matter. In 2025, researchers studied
host-associated

242
00:10:59,534 --> 00:11:03,054
microbial DNA across hundreds of mammoth
specimens,

243
00:11:03,284 --> 00:11:05,754
including material far older than the
Wrangel

244
00:11:05,754 --> 00:11:09,044
population. Some bacteria were related to
known

245
00:11:09,044 --> 00:11:12,084
pathogens. Related does not mean
responsible.

246
00:11:12,404 --> 00:11:14,594
The study did not identify a disease

247
00:11:14,594 --> 00:11:16,824
that killed the Wrangel mammoths, and the

248
00:11:16,824 --> 00:11:19,094
health effects of the ancient microbes
remain

249
00:11:19,094 --> 00:11:23,754
uncertain. Authentication, contamination
control and comparisons with

250
00:11:23,754 --> 00:11:26,214
living relatives can open new questions.

251
00:11:26,624 --> 00:11:28,424
The diagnosis is still missing.

252
00:11:29,590 --> 00:11:30,470
What can we say?

253
00:11:31,050 --> 00:11:33,780
Supported: the island population went
through a

254
00:11:33,780 --> 00:11:37,120
severe bottleneck, followed by a modelled
recovery.

255
00:11:37,390 --> 00:11:41,110
The sampled genomes show reduced
diversity, increased

256
00:11:41,110 --> 00:11:43,940
homozygosity and persistent genetic
strain.

257
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:48,250
Important dietary isotope indicators were
broadly stable

258
00:11:48,250 --> 00:11:51,080
in the samples; other signals were
interpreted

259
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:53,440
as possible changes in water quality.

260
00:11:53,890 --> 00:11:57,230
Plausible, not proven: a severe icing
event,

261
00:11:57,580 --> 00:12:01,530
disease, poorer water, human involvement,
or a

262
00:12:01,530 --> 00:12:04,390
combination of pressures affecting a small
isolated

263
00:12:04,390 --> 00:12:08,400
population. Not established: a single
named killer,

264
00:12:08,850 --> 00:12:11,680
a directly recorded fatal winter, or a

265
00:12:11,680 --> 00:12:13,830
genetic test proving the cause.

266
00:12:14,230 --> 00:12:16,650
The final generations are absent from the

267
00:12:16,650 --> 00:12:20,100
larger genome dataset. A possibility is
not

268
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:22,420
a verdict. The evidence does not show

269
00:12:22,620 --> 00:12:24,730
the herd was always healthy, that people

270
00:12:24,730 --> 00:12:27,150
definitely arrived too late to matter, or

271
00:12:27,150 --> 00:12:29,140
that the final decline followed one known

272
00:12:29,140 --> 00:12:31,510
pattern. These categories do not have to

273
00:12:31,510 --> 00:12:34,500
compete. Vulnerability and a sudden event
could

274
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:37,180
work together, but that is a hypothesis,

275
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:39,580
not the answer to this particular case.

276
00:12:40,010 --> 00:12:45,170
The 2017 genome, the isotope record,
archaeology

277
00:12:45,410 --> 00:12:49,300
and the 2024 genomes each provide
different

278
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:51,700
pieces. A dramatic clue is not the

279
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:53,000
